**Idea:** The baseline evaluates T_k by the linear recurrence, so depth grows to 8. I use the product identities T_{m+n} = 2·T_m·T_n − T_{|m−n|} instead, which keeps the same six ciphertext multiplications but reaches each T_k in fewer levels. T2 and T4 come from squarings, T3 = 2·z·T2 − z, T5 = 2·T2·T3 − z, T6 = 2·T3² − 1 and T7 = 2·T3·T4 − z. The maximum depth of any T_k is 4, so with the final coefficient multiply the circuit uses about 5 levels instead of 8. The coefficient multiplies and the final sum are unchanged.

```cpp
// Chebyshev sigmoid degree 7 on [-4,4], using product identities
// T_{m+n} = 2 T_m T_n - T_{|m-n|} to keep multiplicative depth low.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;            // T1, depth 1

  Ciphertext zz = z * z;               // depth 2
  Ciphertext T2 = zz + zz - one;       // T2

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;             // depth 3
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;       // T3

  Ciphertext T2s = T2 * T2;            // depth 3
  Ciphertext T4 = T2s + T2s - one;     // T4

  Ciphertext T2T3 = T2 * T3;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext T5 = T2T3 + T2T3 - z;     // T5

  Ciphertext T3s = T3 * T3;            // depth 4
  Ciphertext T6 = T3s + T3s - one;     // T6 (unused in the sum, so dropped below)

  Ciphertext T3T4 = T3 * T4;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext T7 = T3T4 + T3T4 - z;     // T7

  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  Ciphertext term1 = z * c1;
  Ciphertext term3 = T3 * c3;
  Ciphertext term5 = T5 * c5;
  Ciphertext term7 = T7 * c7;

  Ciphertext result = term1 + term3;
  result = result + term5;
  result = result + term7;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

**Idea:** The scale mismatch came from two level-5 terms (`term5` and `term7`) being added together, and their multiplication histories differ. I restructure `c5·T5` as `2·(c5·T2)·T3 − c5·z`, which brings it down to level 4. I then add the terms in an order where at most one level-5 term meets an accumulator at a different level, so no two same-level sums have mismatched scales. The depth stays at 5, and the six ciphertext multiplications are unchanged.

```cpp
// Chebyshev sigmoid degree 7 on [-4,4].
// T_{m+n} = 2 T_m T_n - T_{|m-n|}; c5*T5 is formed as 2*((c5*T2)*T3) - c5*z
// so that its level is 4 instead of 5, avoiding same-level scale mismatches.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;            // T1, depth 1

  Ciphertext zz = z * z;               // depth 2
  Ciphertext T2 = zz + zz - one;       // T2

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;             // depth 3
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;       // T3

  Ciphertext T2s = T2 * T2;            // depth 3
  Ciphertext T4 = T2s + T2s - one;     // T4

  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  // c1*T1 and c3*T3
  Ciphertext term1 = z * c1;
  Ciphertext term3 = T3 * c3;

  // c5*T5 = 2*((c5*T2)*T3) - c5*z   (level 4)
  Ciphertext c5T2 = T2 * c5;           // depth 3
  Ciphertext P5 = c5T2 * T3;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext c5z = z * c5;             // depth 2
  Ciphertext term5 = P5 + P5 - c5z;

  // c7*T7 = 2*T3*(T4*c7) - c7*z   (level 5)
  Ciphertext T4c7 = T4 * c7;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext P7 = T3 * T4c7;           // depth 5
  Ciphertext c7z = z * c7;             // depth 2
  Ciphertext term7 = P7 + P7 - c7z;

  Ciphertext result = term1 + term3;
  result = result + term7;
  result = result + term5;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

**Idea:** Attempt 2 was correct but slower because it used 7 ciphertext-plaintext multiplies. I fold the coefficient into a single multiply for `c7·T7` as `c7·(2·T3·T4 − z)`, which takes one ct-pt multiply instead of two. `c5·T5` keeps the level-4 form `2·((c5·T2)·T3) − c5·z`. This gives 5 ct-pt multiplies and 5 ct-ct multiplies, with depth 5. The accumulation order from attempt 2 is kept, since it passed.

```cpp
// Chebyshev sigmoid degree 7 on [-4,4].
// T_{m+n} = 2 T_m T_n - T_{|m-n|}.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;            // T1, depth 1

  Ciphertext zz = z * z;               // depth 2
  Ciphertext T2 = zz + zz - one;       // T2

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;             // depth 3
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;       // T3

  Ciphertext T2s = T2 * T2;            // depth 3
  Ciphertext T4 = T2s + T2s - one;     // T4

  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  Ciphertext term1 = z * c1;           // level 2
  Ciphertext term3 = T3 * c3;          // level 4

  // c5*T5 = 2*((c5*T2)*T3) - c5*z   (level 4)
  Ciphertext c5T2 = T2 * c5;           // depth 3
  Ciphertext P5 = c5T2 * T3;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext c5z = z * c5;             // depth 2
  Ciphertext term5 = P5 + P5 - c5z;

  // c7*T7 = c7*(2*T3*T4 - z)   (level 5, single ct-pt multiply)
  Ciphertext P7 = T3 * T4;             // depth 4
  Ciphertext W7 = P7 + P7 - z;         // T7
  Ciphertext term7 = W7 * c7;          // depth 5

  Ciphertext result = term1 + term3;
  result = result + term7;
  result = result + term5;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

**Idea:** The cost is dominated by ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies, and attempt 3 still used 5. I use the Chebyshev product identity with a degree-4 split: T5 = 2·T4·T1 − T3 and T7 = 2·T4·T3 − T1. Then c5·T5 + c7·T7 = T4·(2c5·z + 2c7·T3) − c5·T3 − c7·z. This needs only four ciphertext multiplies in total (T2, T3, T4, and the single product with T4), at depth 5. The remaining terms are plaintext multiplies on z and T3, which are cheaper.

```cpp
// Chebyshev sigmoid degree 7 on [-4,4].
// Uses T_{4+j} = 2 T4 T_j - T_{4-j}:
//   c5 T5 + c7 T7 = T4 * (2 c5 z + 2 c7 T3) - c5 T3 - c7 z
// so only 4 ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies are needed.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;            // T1, depth 1

  Ciphertext zz = z * z;               // depth 2
  Ciphertext T2 = zz + zz - one;       // T2

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;             // depth 3
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;       // T3

  Ciphertext T2s = T2 * T2;            // depth 3
  Ciphertext T4 = T2s + T2s - one;     // T4

  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  // Quotient Q = 2*c5*z + 2*c7*T3   (depth 4)
  Ciphertext c5z = z * c5;             // depth 2
  Ciphertext c7T3 = T3 * c7;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext Q = (c5z + c5z) + (c7T3 + c7T3);

  // Q * T4  (depth 5)
  Ciphertext P = Q * T4;

  // Remainder pieces
  Ciphertext c1z = z * c1;             // depth 2
  Ciphertext c7z = z * c7;             // depth 2
  Ciphertext A = c1z - c7z;

  Ciphertext c3T3 = T3 * c3;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext c5T3 = T3 * c5;           // depth 4
  Ciphertext B = c3T3 - c5T3;

  Ciphertext result = P + A;
  result = result + B;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

**Idea:** Attempt 4 is the fastest correct kernel so far (149.55 ms vs. 161.56 ms baseline), with four ciphertext multiplies and depth 5. I re-checked the alternatives. Degree 7 needs T2, T3, T4 and one product with T4, so four ciphertext multiplies is the floor for this split. Replacing the T4 split with a T3-based one still needs T4, so it doesn't reduce the count. The remaining plaintext multiplies are scalar-cheap, so I keep the attempt 4 kernel unchanged.

